    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making \"cold.\" A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. That heat escapes through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge. As the refrigerant cools and turns back into a liquid, it cycles around again. This loop keeps repeating, pulling heat out of the food compartment and releasing it into your kitchen. That's why the back of a fridge feels warm—it's dumping the heat it stole from your food!"
